# Proposed wiring and connection diagram

**This is a proposed implementation. It does not represent confirmed original wiring. Verify the exact module pin labels and datasheets before connecting power.**

## Signal connections

| Device | Module pin | Arduino Uno pin | Power / ground | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| TCS34725 breakout | SDA | A4 | VCC to supported supply; GND to common logic ground | I²C data |
| TCS34725 breakout | SCL | A5 | Same as above | I²C clock |
| Digital IR sensor | OUT | D2 | VCC to module-rated supply; GND common | Code assumes LOW = detected; change if opposite |
| Start button | One terminal | D3 | Other terminal to GND | Uses `INPUT_PULLUP` |
| Stop button | One terminal | D4 | Other terminal to GND | Software stop only, not a safety-rated emergency stop |
| L298N | ENA | D5 | Driver motor supply per module; GND common | Remove ENA jumper if PWM control is needed |
| L298N | IN1 | D7 | Same driver supply arrangement | Direction input |
| L298N | IN2 | D8 | Same driver supply arrangement | Direction input |
| SG90 servo | Signal | D9 | Servo power from suitable regulated supply; GND common | Do not power servo from Arduino GPIO |
| Green LED | Anode via 220–330 Ω resistor | D10 | Cathode to GND | Current-limiting resistor required |
| Red LED | Anode via 220–330 Ω resistor | D11 | Cathode to GND | Current-limiting resistor required |
| DC motor | Motor terminals | L298N OUT1/OUT2 | Motor supply to driver motor input | Never connect motor directly to Arduino pins |

## Power notes

1. Use a motor supply matching the motor voltage and stall current; verify the driver's current and thermal ratings.
2. Use a regulated supply for the servo sized for its peak current. Small servos can draw large transient currents.
3. The colour sensor and IR module must be powered at voltages compatible with their exact breakout boards.
4. Connect grounds together for the Arduino, sensor modules, motor driver logic and servo supply where required for signal reference. Keep high motor current out of small signal ground paths.
5. Do not feed an external 5 V supply into the Arduino 5V pin while USB is connected unless the power arrangement is designed to prevent backfeeding. Prefer a documented power plan.
6. Add suitable fuse/overcurrent protection to motor and supply branches.
7. A software button cannot replace a physical, appropriately rated motor power disconnect or safety-rated emergency stop.

## Simplified connection view

```text
TCS34725 SDA ───────── Arduino A4
TCS34725 SCL ───────── Arduino A5
IR sensor OUT ──────── Arduino D2
Start button ───────── Arduino D3 ── button to GND
Stop button ────────── Arduino D4 ── button to GND
Arduino D5/D7/D8 ───── L298N ENA/IN1/IN2
L298N OUT1/OUT2 ────── DC motor
Arduino D9 ─────────── Servo signal
Arduino D10 ─ resistor ─ green LED ─ GND
Arduino D11 ─ resistor ─ red LED ─── GND

Motor supply ───────── L298N motor power input
Regulated logic supply ─ Arduino / sensors as compatible
Regulated servo supply ─ servo V+
All signal grounds ─── common reference ground
```

## Important mechanical note

The example program stops the conveyor while colour is measured. This reduces motion blur and simplifies the prototype. The servo angles and gate geometry are examples only; mechanically test the gate with lightweight dummy objects before using tomatoes.
